<BaseLayout
  lang="en"
  title="The Nominal Sentence (al-jumla al-ismiyya) in Arabic Grammar · Irab App"
  description="A full lesson on al-jumla al-ismiyya: its two pillars mubtadaʾ and khabar, why both are marfūʿ without any verb, the three kinds of khabar, fronting the predicate, indefinite subjects, and the abrogators, with complete classical iʿrāb and Quranic evidence."
>
  <ArticleSchema slot="head" lang="en" kind="lesson" headline="The Nominal Sentence (al-jumla al-ismiyya)" description="A full lesson on al-jumla al-ismiyya: its two pillars mubtadaʾ and khabar, why both are marfūʿ without any verb, the three kinds of khabar, fronting the predicate, indefinite subjects, and the abrogators, with complete classical iʿrāb and Quranic evidence." />
  <Nav lang="en" />

  <div class="en-lesson">
    <ArticleHero
      lang="en"
      title="The Nominal Sentence"
      summary="Arabic builds statements in two ways, and everything in naḥw follows from which one you are looking at. The nominal sentence, al-jumla al-ismiyya, opens with a noun and rests on two pillars: the mubtadaʾ and the khabar, both in the nominative. This lesson explains what puts them there when there is no verb in sight, the three shapes the predicate can take, when the order reverses, and what happens when an abrogator enters."
    />
    <Byline lang="en" kind="lesson" />
    <LessonNav slug={slug} place="top" />

    <ArticleSection
      lang="en"
      id="terms"
      title="The terms, before anything else"
      lead="Half the difficulty English speakers have with Arabic grammar comes from English labels that almost fit. Fix the vocabulary first and the rules become straightforward."
    >
      <LessonObjective items={objectives} />
      <ul>
        <li><strong>الجملة الاسمية</strong>, <em>al-jumla al-ismiyya</em>, "the nominal sentence": a sentence whose opening element is a noun.</li>
        <li><strong>المبتدأ</strong>, <em>al-mubtadaʾ</em>, literally "that which is begun with", usually glossed <em>subject</em>. <strong>Handle with care:</strong> English also calls the doer of a verb the "subject", but Arabic gives that a different name entirely, <em>fāʿil</em>, in a different sentence type. A mubtadaʾ is not a fāʿil, and the two are never interchangeable.</li>
        <li><strong>الخبر</strong>, <em>al-khabar</em>, "the report, the information", glossed <em>predicate</em>. It is what you are saying <em>about</em> the mubtadaʾ.</li>
        <li><strong>مرفوع</strong>, <em>marfūʿ</em>, "raised": the nominative state. Its default marker is <em>ḍamma</em>, the vowel ‑u.</li>
        <li><strong>عامل</strong>, <em>ʿāmil</em>, "governor, operator": whatever causes a word to take the case it takes. Governance is the engine of the whole system.</li>
        <li><strong>رابط</strong>, <em>rābiṭ</em>, "link": the element tying a sentence-predicate back to its subject.</li>
        <li><strong>نواسخ</strong>, <em>nawāsikh</em>, "abrogators": words that enter the nominal sentence and overturn its default case pattern.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Arabic terms are given in script, then transliterated, then glossed. The transliteration is academic: macrons mark long vowels, <em>ʿ</em> is ʿayn and <em>ʾ</em> is hamza, so every form can be read back into Arabic without ambiguity.</p>
    </ArticleSection>

    <ArticleSection
      lang="en"
      id="definition"
      title="Learn: what makes a sentence nominal"
      lead="A sentence is nominal when the first of its original elements is a noun. That is the whole test, and it is a test about form, not about meaning."
    >
      <p>The grammarians classify by the <em>ṣadr</em>, the opening of the sentence.</p>

      <ExampleCard
        label="Example 1"
        ar="مُحَمَّدٌ مُسَافِرٌ"
        focus="مُحَمَّدٌ"
        translit="Muḥammadun musāfirun"
        en="Muhammad is travelling."
        irab="محمدٌ مبتدأ مرفوع وعلامة رفعه الضمة الظاهرة على آخره، مسافرٌ خبر مرفوع وعلامة رفعه الضمة الظاهرة على آخره."
        note="Nominal, because the opening element is a noun. Both pillars carry ḍamma, and no verb appears anywhere in the sentence."
      />

      <p>The case that surprises English speakers is a sentence that plainly contains a verb and is still nominal.</p>

      <ExampleCard
        label="Example 2, the one that catches people out"
        ar="الطَّالِبُ كَتَبَ الدَّرْسَ"
        focus="الطَّالِبُ"
        translit="al-ṭālibu kataba l-darsa"
        en="The student wrote the lesson."
        irab="الطالبُ مبتدأ مرفوع وعلامة رفعه الضمة الظاهرة، كتبَ فعل ماضٍ مبني على الفتح، والفاعل ضمير مستتر تقديره هو، الدرسَ مفعول به منصوب وعلامة نصبه الفتحة الظاهرة، والجملة الفعلية في محل رفع خبر المبتدأ."
        note="There is a verb here, yet the sentence is nominal, because a noun came first. The noun is therefore a mubtadaʾ, and the whole verbal sentence after it occupies the slot of the khabar. Compare kataba l-ṭālibu l-darsa, which opens with the verb and is verbal, and in which al-ṭālibu is a fāʿil, not a mubtadaʾ. Same words, same English translation, two different analyses."
      />

      <p>This classification is Baṣran, and it is the one the mainstream tradition and this site follow. The Kūfans analysed fronted-noun sentences differently in places; where such disagreements affect the iʿrāb you will meet them in the relevant lesson.</p>
      <p>Semantically the tradition observes that the nominal sentence tends to express <em>thubūt</em>, a settled state, while the verbal sentence expresses <em>tajaddud</em>, renewal and occurrence. That is a tendency worth knowing, not a rule to parse by. Parse by the opening element.</p>

      <CheckQuestion
        q="Is this sentence nominal or verbal, and what is the grammatical role of the first word?"
        ar="الْمُعَلِّمُونَ يَشْرَحُونَ الدَّرْسَ"
        answer="Nominal. al-muʿallimūna is a mubtadaʾ, not a fāʿil."
        why="A noun opens the sentence, so the sentence is nominal regardless of the verb that follows. The verbal sentence yashraḥūna l-darsa then sits in the position of khabar. Had the verb come first, yashraḥu l-muʿallimūna l-darsa, the noun would have been a fāʿil."
      />
    </ArticleSection>

    <ArticleSection
      lang="en"
      id="the-two-pillars"
      title="Understand: what raises the two pillars"
      lead="Both mubtadaʾ and khabar are marfūʿ. The interesting question, and the one most textbooks skip, is what puts them in the nominative when the sentence contains no verb to govern anything."
    >
      <ExampleCard
        label="The base case"
        ar="الْعِلْمُ نُورٌ"
        focus={['الْعِلْمُ', 'نُورٌ']}
        translit="al-ʿilmu nūrun"
        en="Knowledge is light."
        irab="العلمُ مبتدأ مرفوع وعلامة رفعه الضمة الظاهرة على آخره، نورٌ خبر مرفوع وعلامة رفعه الضمة الظاهرة على آخره."
        note="Notice the shape of the statement: what the word is, what case it is in, which marker shows that case, and why that marker and not another. Every iʿrāb line you will ever read has these parts."
      />

      <p>Now the governance question. In a verbal sentence the verb is the ʿāmil: it raises its fāʿil. Here there is no verb. The grammarians' answer is that the mubtadaʾ is raised by <em>al-ibtidāʾ</em> itself, by the bare fact of being the element the sentence is built on. This is a <em>ʿāmil maʿnawī</em>, an abstract governor rather than a word you can point to. The khabar in turn is raised by the mubtadaʾ. So the chain runs: initiality raises the subject, and the subject raises the predicate.</p>
      <p>This matters practically. Students who have learned that "the verb causes the endings" go looking for a verb in <em>al-ʿilmu nūrun</em>, fail to find one, and guess. There is nothing to find. The governor here is a relationship, not a word.</p>

      <ExampleCard
        label="The same case, a different marker"
        ar="الْمُؤْمِنُونَ صَادِقُونَ"
        focus={['الْمُؤْمِنُونَ', 'صَادِقُونَ']}
        translit="al-muʾminūna ṣādiqūna"
        en="The believers are truthful."
        irab="المؤمنون مبتدأ مرفوع وعلامة رفعه الواو لأنه جمع مذكر سالم، صادقون خبر مرفوع وعلامة رفعه الواو لأنه جمع مذكر سالم."
        note="Both are still marfūʿ, but the marker is wāw rather than ḍamma, because a sound masculine plural takes letters as case markers instead of vowels. The case never changed; only its outward sign did."
      />

      <p>The markers themselves, original and substitute, are the subject of <a href="/en/duroos/alamat-al-irab">the markers of iʿrāb</a>.</p>
    </ArticleSection>

    <ArticleSection
      lang="en"
      id="english-contrast"
      title="What English can and cannot lend you"
      lead="English has almost no case system left, but the fragment it retains is exactly the right analogy, and there is one habit it will actively mislead you into."
    >
      <p>English marks case on pronouns only: <em>he</em> against <em>him</em>, <em>who</em> against <em>whom</em>. Nobody thinks "him" means something different from "he"; it is the same word wearing a different grammatical role. Arabic simply does openly, on nearly every noun, what English does on a handful of pronouns. When you read <em>al-ʿilmu</em> and later <em>al-ʿilma</em>, that final vowel is doing the work that <em>he</em> and <em>him</em> do.</p>
      <p>The habit to unlearn is supplying "is". "Knowledge <strong>is</strong> light" needs a verb in English; the Arabic has none, and no word in it corresponds to "is". If you go looking for the copula you will invent a governor that is not there. The equational relationship is carried by two nouns standing together, both in the nominative.</p>
      <p>Learners who know Latin, German, Greek or Russian have a real advantage and should lean on it: marfūʿ is close to nominative, manṣūb to accusative, majrūr to genitive. The correspondence is close enough to be useful and loose enough to be dangerous, so treat it as a bridge to intuition rather than a definition.</p>
    </ArticleSection>

    <ArticleSection
      lang="en"
      id="types-of-khabar"
      title="See: the three shapes of the khabar"
      lead="The predicate can be a single word, a whole sentence, or a prepositional or adverbial phrase. Recognising which one you are looking at is most of the work of parsing a nominal sentence."
    >
      <p><strong>1. Mufrad, a single word.</strong> Note that <em>mufrad</em> here is technical, meaning "not a sentence and not a phrase". A dual or a plural still counts as mufrad in this sense.</p>

      <ExampleCard
        label="Khabar mufrad"
        ar="الصِّدْقُ فَضِيلَةٌ"
        focus="فَضِيلَةٌ"
        translit="al-ṣidqu faḍīlatun"
        en="Truthfulness is a virtue."
        irab="الصدقُ مبتدأ مرفوع وعلامة رفعه الضمة الظاهرة، فضيلةٌ خبر مرفوع وعلامة رفعه الضمة الظاهرة."
      />

      <p><strong>2. Jumla, a whole sentence.</strong> A sentence-predicate must contain a <strong>rābiṭ</strong> tying it to the subject, or the two halves are unrelated statements sitting side by side.</p>

      <ExampleCard
        label="Khabar jumla"
        ar="الْحَدِيقَةُ أَزْهَارُهَا جَمِيلَةٌ"
        focus="أَزْهَارُهَا"
        translit="al-ḥadīqatu azhāruhā jamīlatun"
        en="The garden, its flowers are beautiful."
        irab="الحديقةُ مبتدأ أول مرفوع، أزهارُ مبتدأ ثانٍ مرفوع وهو مضاف، والهاء ضمير متصل مبني في محل جر بالإضافة، جميلةٌ خبر المبتدأ الثاني مرفوع، والجملة الاسمية من المبتدأ الثاني وخبره في محل رفع خبر المبتدأ الأول، والرابط الضمير."
        note="A complete nominal sentence is itself serving as the predicate of al-ḥadīqatu. The phrase fī maḥall rafʿ, 'in the position of nominative', is how the tradition assigns a case to something that cannot carry a vowel: a whole sentence has no final letter to mark, but it occupies a slot that is nominative. The link here is the pronoun -hā in azhāruhā, 'its flowers'."
      />

      <p><strong>3. Shibh jumla, a quasi-sentence.</strong> This is the subtle one, and the analysis is not what the English translation suggests.</p>

      <ExampleCard
        label="Khabar shibh jumla"
        ar="الْكِتَابُ فَوْقَ الطَّاوِلَةِ"
        focus="فَوْقَ الطَّاوِلَةِ"
        translit="al-kitābu fawqa l-ṭāwilati"
        en="The book is on the table."
        irab="الكتابُ مبتدأ مرفوع وعلامة رفعه الضمة الظاهرة، فوقَ ظرف مكان منصوب وعلامة نصبه الفتحة الظاهرة وهو مضاف، الطاولةِ مضاف إليه مجرور وعلامة جره الكسرة الظاهرة، وشبه الجملة متعلق بمحذوف خبر."
        note="The tradition does not say the phrase is the predicate. It says the phrase is mutaʿalliq bi-maḥdhūf khabar, 'attached to an omitted predicate'. The reasoning: a predicate must be a noun or a sentence, and a prepositional phrase is neither, so an implied predicate such as kāʾinun, 'being, located', is posited and the phrase attaches to it. English translations quietly collapse this, but the omitted element is what the analysis rests on, and it reappears in many later lessons."
      />

      <CheckQuestion
        q="Which of the three kinds of khabar is this, and what is the link?"
        ar="الْمُسْلِمُ أَخْلَاقُهُ حَسَنَةٌ"
        answer="A sentence-predicate (khabar jumla). The link is the attached pronoun -hu in akhlāquhu."
        why="akhlāquhu ḥasanatun is itself a complete nominal sentence occupying the position of khabar for al-muslimu, fī maḥall rafʿ. Remove the pronoun and nothing would tie the two halves together."
      />
    </ArticleSection>

    <ArticleSection
      lang="en"
      id="word-order"
      title="Reversing the order, and starting with an indefinite"
      lead="The default is mubtadaʾ then khabar. The order may reverse, sometimes optionally and sometimes obligatorily, and one of those obligatory cases is also what licenses an indefinite subject."
    >
      <p>Fronting the khabar is <strong>obligatory</strong> in three well known situations:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>The khabar is a shibh jumla and the mubtadaʾ is indefinite.</li>
        <li>The khabar has an inherent right to initial position, such as an interrogative: <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">أَيْنَ الْكِتَابُ؟</span>, <em>ayna l-kitābu</em>, "where is the book?"</li>
        <li>The mubtadaʾ carries a pronoun referring to something inside the khabar: <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">فِي الدَّارِ صَاحِبُهَا</span>, <em>fī l-dāri ṣāḥibuhā</em>, "in the house is its owner". Leaving the order unreversed would make the pronoun point forward to something not yet mentioned.</li>
      </ul>

      <ExampleCard
        label="Fronted predicate, postponed subject"
        ar="فِي الدَّارِ رَجُلٌ"
        focus="رَجُلٌ"
        translit="fī l-dāri rajulun"
        en="In the house is a man."
        irab="في حرف جر مبني على السكون لا محل له من الإعراب، الدارِ اسم مجرور بفي وعلامة جره الكسرة الظاهرة، والجار والمجرور متعلقان بمحذوف خبر مقدم، رجلٌ مبتدأ مؤخر مرفوع وعلامة رفعه الضمة الظاهرة."
        note="The prepositional phrase attaches to an omitted fronted predicate, and rajulun is the postponed subject, still nominative. This is also the sentence that licenses an indefinite subject."
      />

      <p>Which brings us to indefinite subjects. The default is a definite mubtadaʾ, for a plain reason: a statement about something entirely unidentified conveys nothing. "A man is tall" tells a listener nothing at all. An indefinite is permitted when something makes it informative, and the tradition catalogues these licensors, <em>musawwighāt</em>:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>It is described:</strong> <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">رَجُلٌ كَرِيمٌ زَارَنَا</span>, <em>rajulun karīmun zāranā</em>, "a generous man visited us".</li>
        <li><strong>It follows negation or a question:</strong> <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">مَا أَحَدٌ عِنْدَنَا</span>, <em>mā aḥadun ʿindanā</em>, "no one is with us".</li>
        <li><strong>It expresses a prayer:</strong> <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">سَلَامٌ عَلَيْكُمْ</span>, <em>salāmun ʿalaykum</em>, "peace be upon you".</li>
        <li><strong>The predicate is fronted:</strong> the case above.</li>
      </ul>
    </ArticleSection>

    <ArticleSection
      lang="en"
      id="nawasikh"
      title="The abrogators: when the pattern is overturned"
      lead="Certain verbs and particles enter the nominal sentence and cancel its default pattern of two nominatives. They are called nawāsikh, abrogators, and the two families pull in opposite directions."
    >
      <ExampleCard
        label="Kāna: predicate to the accusative"
        ar="كَانَ الْجَوُّ صَحْوًا"
        focus="صَحْوًا"
        translit="kāna l-jawwu ṣaḥwan"
        en="The weather was clear."
        irab="كان فعل ماضٍ ناقص مبني على الفتح، الجوُّ اسم كان مرفوع وعلامة رفعه الضمة الظاهرة، صحوًا خبر كان منصوب وعلامة نصبه الفتحة الظاهرة."
        note="Note the renaming: the pillars are no longer mubtadaʾ and khabar but ism kāna and khabar kāna. Kāna is called nāqiṣ, 'incomplete', because unlike an ordinary verb it does not describe an event. It contributes tense and requires a predicate to complete it."
      />

      <ExampleCard
        label="Inna: subject to the accusative"
        ar="إِنَّ اللهَ غَفُورٌ رَحِيمٌ"
        focus="اللهَ"
        translit="inna Llāha ghafūrun raḥīmun"
        en="Indeed Allah is Forgiving, Merciful."
        irab="إنّ حرف توكيد ونصب مبني على الفتح لا محل له من الإعراب، اللهَ لفظ الجلالة اسم إنّ منصوب وعلامة نصبه الفتحة الظاهرة، غفورٌ خبر إنّ مرفوع وعلامة رفعه الضمة الظاهرة، رحيمٌ خبر ثانٍ مرفوع."
        note="Exactly the reverse of kāna: accusative on the first pillar, nominative on the predicate. Note also that a single mubtadaʾ can carry more than one khabar."
      />

      <p>The word <em>nāsikh</em> is worth pausing on: its root means to cancel or supersede. The grammarians are saying the sentence still <em>is</em> a nominal sentence with two pillars. The abrogator has overturned the case ruling on them, not rebuilt the sentence. Each family has its own conditions and meanings, treated in <a href="/en/duroos/kana-wa-akhawatuha">kāna and its sisters</a> and <a href="/en/duroos/inna-wa-akhawatuha">inna and its sisters</a>.</p>

      <CheckQuestion
        q="What case is each pillar in, and what are they called now?"
        ar="إِنَّ الصَّبْرَ مِفْتَاحُ الْفَرَجِ"
        answer="al-ṣabra is manṣūb and is called ism inna; miftāḥu is marfūʿ and is called khabar inna."
        why="Inna puts the first pillar in the accusative and leaves the predicate nominative. Had this been kāna l-ṣabru miftāḥan, the cases would have been the other way round."
      />
    </ArticleSection>

    <ArticleSection
      lang="en"
      id="common-mistakes"
      title="Where English speakers go wrong"
      lead="Four errors account for most mistakes on this chapter, and three of them come specifically from carrying English assumptions across."
    >
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Putting the khabar in the accusative.</strong> Writing <em>al-ʿilmu nūran</em> by analogy with an object. The predicate is nominative always, unless an abrogator has changed it. There is no verb here to take an object.</li>
        <li><strong>Hunting for the missing "is".</strong> Because English needs a copula, learners look for a governor that does not exist and then cannot explain the endings. The governor is <em>al-ibtidāʾ</em>, an abstract one.</li>
        <li><strong>Forgetting the rābiṭ.</strong> When the predicate is a sentence it must contain a pronoun, visible or implied, pointing back at the subject.</li>
        <li><strong>Confusing a postponed mubtadaʾ with a fāʿil.</strong> In <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">فِي الصَّفِّ طَالِبٌ</span>, <em>fī l-ṣaffi ṭālibun</em>, there is no verb in the sentence at all, so <em>ṭālibun</em> cannot be a fāʿil. It is a postponed mubtadaʾ. A fāʿil requires a verb; no verb, no fāʿil, ever.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The cure for all four is the same: parse real sentences, out loud, in the full classical wording, until the shape of the iʿrāb line is automatic. Read the <a href="/en/irab">iʿrāb reference</a> for that wording, then take sentences from anything you are reading and parse them before checking yourself.</p>
    </ArticleSection>

